  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>From the Corniche Escartefigue (or the Mémorial du Débarquement car park for a shorter variant), the trail climbs up to the Fort de la Croix Faron. The climb is sustained over stony ground, but the reward matches the effort: an exceptional panoramic view over the whole of Toulon harbour.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Allow about 6.4 km round trip and 2h30 to 3h of walking. The City's "Toulon, ville amie des chiens" document states that a leash is compulsory throughout the commune, that the Faron massif welcomes dogs and that they ride the cable car free of charge. The cable car leaflet also asks you to keep your dog on a leash, stay on the marked paths and avoid the edges of cliffs and sinkholes. No water point is announced anywhere on the route: bring a generous supply for your dog. Favour spring or autumn, or set off very early in the morning in summer.</p>
      <p>The 400-hectare forest massif, managed by the Toulon Provence Méditerranée Metropole with the ONF, the commune and the REDIF, is subject to fire risk: from 21 June to 20 September, the Metropole asks you to check access restrictions every day on the Var massifs map, and access may be closed, cable car included. Smoking and lighting fires are forbidden all year. The cable car leaflet also specifies that access to the forts is forbidden: stay on the marked paths and outside the buildings.</p>
      <p>At the summit, walking times from the cable car arrival station are 3 minutes to the memorial, 10 to the chapel, 20 to the wildlife park and 35 to the Croix du Faron - Point Sublime. The cable car accepts dogs free of charge if kept on a short leash, in the cabin and throughout the site; the ride lasts about 6 minutes. It may close in strong wind (reception: 04 94 92 68 25). Dogs are banned from the wildlife park and the memorial.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: there's no spring or fountain on this trail, so never set off without a good water bottle for your dog. If you'd rather have the view without the effort, the Mont Faron cable car also works very well: a ride of about 6 minutes, and pets are accepted in the cabin (dogs on a short leash, free according to the Provence Méditerranée tourist office). The cable car closes every year from late November to early February, and dogs are banned from the wildlife park (big cat enclosure) and the memorial.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Toulon harbour is seen from a different angle from the summit. Along the way or once up there: the Chapelle Notre-Dame du Faron rounds out the outing nicely; however, the Mémorial du Débarquement and the big cat enclosure are banned to dogs according to the Faron cable car. Then head back down to the town centre, just a few minutes by car.</p>
    </div>

